on of music through the  human creative mind in all of these  processes there's a common thread a  common theme that we localize  structure we put things in a certain  place we move things at the other place  or we don't even put that beginning  and thereby we create a distribution a  patterning and  the way life emerges is creating  patterns across these 20 amino acids now  these 20 amino acids can be made audible  this information  is stored at very high frequencies and  naturally we cannot hear them our  auditory system  can only hear up to about 20 kilohertz  we can solve this problem by using the  concept of transposition or  transpositional equivalence as this  simple example shows i'm beginning to  play a melody  in the beginning at very high  frequencies and i'm slowly moving down  i'm transposing this  this piece of music into lower and lower  frequencies  until it becomes audible so as you  listen pay close attention and see at  what point you actually recognize  what melody i'm playing  [Music]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  once you have recognized the melody i'm  playing it again i'm transposing it  again to lower frequencies and  to your ear to your brain you can  clearly still identify that this is the  same melody it's four leaves by  beethoven  however i'm playing it at yet lower  frequencies  and this is what we call transposition  equivalence we can play melodies at  different frequency levels  by keeping the ratios the multipliers of  frequencies  the same and thereby we retain musical  information structural information that  is elementary to understanding  through the auditory system in our brain  what kind of sound has been created and  what that means for  for processing in the brain in a similar  way we can use this  to translate proteins into audible  spectrum  and what you hear in the background now  are all these 20 amino acid building  blocks played in a succession and you  can hear that each of the